Here is a simple example of how you can create a Rust application that supports the ElevenLabs.io API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using TTS (Text-to-Speech):
```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs;
use std::io;
use std::path::Path;
use std::process::Command;

use elevenlabs_api::{ElevenLabs, ElevenLabsError};
use tts::{TTS, TTSError};

fn main() -> Result<(), ElevenLabsError> {
    // Set up ElevenLabs API credentials
    let api_key = env::var("ELEVENLABS_API_KEY")?;
    let api_secret = env::var("ELEVENLABS_API_SECRET")?;

    // Create an ElevenLabs API client
    let client = ElevenLabs::new(api_key, api_secret)?;

    // Get the contents of the clipboard
    let clipboard_contents = get_clipboard_contents()?;

    // Use the ElevenLabs API to generate a TTS audio file
    let audio_file = client.generate_tts_audio(clipboard_contents)?;

    // Play the TTS audio file
    play_audio_file(&audio_file)?;

    Ok(())
}

fn get_clipboard_contents() -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    // Use the xclip command to get the contents of the clipboard
    let output = Command::new("xclip")
        .arg("-o")
        .output()?;

    // Return the contents of the clipboard as a string
    String::from_utf8(output.stdout).map_err(|_| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidData, "Failed to parse clipboard contents"))
}

fn play_audio_file(file_path: &str) -> Result<(), TTSError> {
    // Use the aplay command to play the audio file
    let output = Command::new("aplay")
        .arg(file_path)
        .output()?;

    // Return the result of playing the audio file
    if output.status.success() {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(TTSError::new("Failed to play audio file"))
    }
}
```
This code uses the `elevenlabs_api` crate to interact with the ElevenLabs.io API, and the `tts` crate to play the generated TTS audio file. It also uses the `xclip` command to get the contents of the clipboard and the `aplay` command to play the audio file.

You will need to add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:
```toml
[dependencies]
elevenlabs_api = "0.1.0"
tts = "0.1.0"
```
You will also need to set the `ELEVENLABS_API_KEY` and `ELEVENLABS_API_SECRET` environment variables to your ElevenLabs.io API credentials.

Note that this is just a simple example, and you may need to modify it to suit your specific needs. Additionally, you will need to install the `xclip` and `aplay` commands on your system in order to use this code.

